{"cuisine/korean-mexican-fusion":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/korean-mexican-fusion","n":"Korean-Mexican fusion","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cali-Mex cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-california-latino-and-hispanic-influence"],["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california"],["Korean-American cuisine","cuisine/korean-americans-cuisine"],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["Korean–Mexican fusion is a type of fusion cuisine originally from Los Angeles that combines traditional elements of American-style Mexican and Korean foods. The earliest Korean-Mexican fusion featured Mexican or Tex-Mex dishes such as tacos or burritos filled with Korean-style barbecued meats and kimchi. Typical dishes include Korean tacos and bulgogi burritos. Food critics Jane and Michael Stern state that Korean–Mexican fusion is a growing food trend that has steadily gained popularity since 2009."],["Restaurants serving Korean tacos have existed in the United States at least since 1996, with a restaurant in Santa Monica, California called \"2424 Pico\" using the name for Korean fillings inside of a lettuce wrap. The proprietors of the Kogi Korean BBQ, a food truck in Los Angeles, California, used Twitter to announce their schedule and itinerary.  In the following years, food trucks serving Korean–Mexican fusion opened in several cities across the U.S., including San Francisco, Austin, Chicago, Portland, Seattle and Washington. after the first food truck serving Korean tacos, Kogi Korean BBQ, opened in Los Angeles in late 2008."],["Several restaurants serving Korean–Mexican fusion exist in Seoul, South Korea and Beijing, China. Unlike in the United States, Korean-Mexican fusion exists in the casual dining segment, while street food trucks continue to dominate in the US."],["Typical dishes include tacos or burritos prepared with Korean barbecue, such as kalbi or spicy chicken, and American foods such as french fries with kimchi."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"korean-mexican-fusion"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean-Mexican_fusion"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Cali-Mex cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-california-latino-and-hispanic-influence"],["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california"],["Korean-American cuisine","cuisine/korean-americans-cuisine"],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"L.A. Son: My Life, My City, My Food","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Roy Choi with Tien Nguyen and Natasha Phan, 2013","note":"Memoir and recipes from the Los Angeles chef behind the Kogi Korean taco trucks.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780062202635","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780062202635"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Cali-Mex cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-california-latino-and-hispanic-influence"],["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california"],["Korean-American cuisine","cuisine/korean-americans-cuisine"],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/77/Korean_tacos.jpg/500px-Korean_tacos.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"insatiablemunch","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean_tacos.jpg"}},"ingredient/zest":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/zest","n":"Zest","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Zest is a food ingredient that is prepared by scraping or cutting from the rind of unwaxed citrus fruits such as lemon, orange, citron, and lime. Zest is used to add flavor to many different types of food."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-zest"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zest_(ingredient)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Chelsea bun","dish/chelsea-bun"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"]]],["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine",[["Oliebol","dish/oliebol"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Crêpes Suzette","dish/crepes-suzette"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Crêpes Suzette","dish/crepes-suzette"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Nutmeg","ingredient/nutmeg"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"],["Zante currant","ingredient/zante-currant"],["Apple","ingredient/apple"],["Berry","ingredient/berry"],["Bread crumbs","ingredient/bread-crumbs"],["Breadcrumbs","ingredient/breadcrumbs"],["Pear","ingredient/pear"],["Currant","ingredient/currant"],["Caramel","ingredient/caramel"],["Crêpe","ingredient/crepe"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c3/Zesting_an_orange.jpg/500px-Zesting_an_orange.jpg","w":500,"h":412,"by":"Dvortygirl","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zesting_an_orange.jpg"}},"ingredient/peel":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/peel","n":"Peel","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Peel, also known as rind or skin, is the outer protective layer of a fruit or vegetable which can be peeled off. The rind is usually the botanical exocarp, but the term exocarp also includes the hard cases of nuts, which are not named peels since they are not peeled off by hand or peeler, but rather shells because of their hardness."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-peel"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peel_(fruit)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Czech cuisine","cuisine/czech-cuisine",[["Buchteln","dish/buchteln"]]],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine",[["Buchteln","dish/buchteln"]]],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia",[["Buchteln","dish/buchteln"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Curd","ingredient/curd"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Fruit preserves","ingredient/fruit-preserves"],["Jam","ingredient/jam"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/bf/Orange_zest_and_peel.jpg/500px-Orange_zest_and_peel.jpg","w":500,"h":459,"by":"Dvortygirl","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orange_zest_and_peel.jpg"}},"ingredient/pecorino-romano":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pecorino-romano","n":"Pecorino romano","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Pecorino romano is a hard, salty Italian cheese made from sheep milk, often used for grating over pasta or other dishes. The name pecorino means 'ovine' or 'of sheep' in Italian; the name of the cheese, although protected, is a description rather than a brand: [formaggio] pecorino romano means 'sheep's [cheese] of Rome'."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pecorino-romano"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pecorino_romano"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"]]],["Lazian or Roman","cuisine/roman-cuisine",[["Cacio e pepe","dish/cacio-e-pepe"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Spaghetti","ingredient/spaghetti"],["Pecorino Romano PDO","ingredient/pecorino-romano-pdo"],["Spaghetti alla chitarra","ingredient/spaghetti-alla-chitarra"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Guanciale","ingredient/guanciale"],["Pancetta","ingredient/pancetta"],["Pecorino","ingredient/pecorino"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ee/Pecorino_romano_cheese.jpg/500px-Pecorino_romano_cheese.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Jon Sullivan","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pecorino_romano_cheese.jpg"}},"ingredient/eel-as-food":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/eel-as-food","n":"Eel as food","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Eels are elongated fish, ranging in length from five centimetres (2 in) to four metres (13 ft), and are used in various cuisines around the world. Japan consumes more than 70 percent of the global eel catch."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-eel-as-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eel_as_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine",[["Eel soup","dish/eel-soup"]]],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia",[["Eel soup","dish/eel-soup"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["European eel","ingredient/european-eel"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/51/Confectionery_shop_and_eel_shop_by_Hidehiro_Komatsu.jpg/500px-Confectionery_shop_and_eel_shop_by_Hidehiro_Komatsu.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Hidehiro Komatsu from Kyoto","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confectionery_shop_and_eel_shop_by_Hidehiro_Komatsu.jpg"}},"ingredient/european-eel":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/european-eel","n":"European eel","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The European eel (Anguilla anguilla) is a species of eel. Their life history was a mystery for thousands of years, and mating in the wild has not yet been observed. The five stages of their development were originally thought to be different species. They are critically endangered due to hydroelectric dams, overfishing by fisheries on coasts for human consumption, and parasites."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-european-eel"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_eel"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine",[["Eel soup","dish/eel-soup"]]],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia",[["Eel soup","dish/eel-soup"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Eel as food","ingredient/eel-as-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/5/58/Anguilla_anguilla.jpg","w":500,"h":331,"by":"GerardM","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anguilla_anguilla.jpg"}},"ingredient/gram-flour":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/gram-flour","n":"Gram flour","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Chickpea flour, common varieties include besan or gram flour, is a pulse flour made from chana dal or chickpea flour (split Bengal gram) or brown/kaala chana, a chickpea.  It is a staple ingredient in cuisines of the Indian subcontinent, including Indian, Bangladeshi, Burmese, Nepali, Pakistani, and Sri Lankan cuisines. It is present in the closely related Caribbean cuisines, as well as in Lunigiana cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-gram-flour"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gram_flour"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Bhojpuri cuisine","cuisine/bhojpuri-cuisine",[["Litti (dish)","dish/litti-dish"]]],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Mirchi bada","dish/mirchi-bada"]]],["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine",[["Haggis pakora","dish/haggis-pakora"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Besan","ingredient/besan"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Haggis","ingredient/haggis"],["Oatmeal","ingredient/oatmeal"],["Offal","ingredient/offal"],["Suet","ingredient/suet"],["Yogurt","ingredient/yogurt"],["Atta","ingredient/atta"],["Brinjal","ingredient/brinjal"],["Cilantro","ingredient/cilantro"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Mustard oil","ingredient/mustard-oil"],["Sattu","ingredient/sattu"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5e/Gram_flour_AvL.jpg/500px-Gram_flour_AvL.jpg","w":500,"h":331,"by":"AlexanderVanLoon","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gram_flour_AvL.jpg"}},"dish/imqaret":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/imqaret","n":"Imqaret","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Maltese cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Imqaret are traditional Maltese sweets made with pastry and a filling of dates. The word imqaret in Maltese, is the plural of maqrut (diamond-shaped) and it signifies the diamond shape of the sweets – even though in many cases they are sold in a rectangular shape. They are very popular in Malta and they are sold in street markets, as well as in village feasts, in some cases served with ice-cream."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-imqaret"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imqaret"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Maltese cuisine","cuisine/maltese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Maltese cuisine","links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Maltese bread","dish/maltese-bread"],["Pastizz","dish/pastizz"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3d/Homemade_Imqaret.jpg/500px-Homemade_Imqaret.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Simon Cutajar","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homemade_Imqaret.jpg"}},"dish/katt-bafla":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/katt-bafla","n":"Katt bafla","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Rajasthani cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Katt bafla is a popular and traditional dish from the Kota and Bundi districts of Hadoti region in Rajasthan, India. It is a famous dish across the north Indian states in India. Traditionally this dish is made on every occasion in Rajasthan. Bafla is a type of spherical wheat bread whereas the katt is a type of barfi made with a mixture of unsalted wheat bread coarse powder with ghee and jaggery powder. Traditionally this dish is served with daal and kadhi."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-katt-bafla"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katt_bafla"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Rajasthani cuisine","cuisine/rajasthani-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Jaggery","ingredient/jaggery"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"My Ginger Garlic Kitchen (Rajasthani)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mygingergarlickitchen.com","note":"Rajasthani section of Anupama Paliwal's Indian home cooking blog.","url":"https://www.mygingergarlickitchen.com/categories/rajasthani-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Tarla Dalal (Sindhi)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cdn.tarladalal.com","note":"Rajasthani category on the recipe site of the late Indian cookbook author Tarla Dalal.","url":"https://cdn.tarladalal.com/recipes/category/Rajasthani-Food","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Rajasthani cuisine","links":[["Dal dhokli","dish/dal-dhokli"]]}]},"dish/lampredotto":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/lampredotto","n":"Lampredotto","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Lampredotto is a typical Florentine dish, made from the fourth and final stomach of cattle, the abomasum."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-lampredotto"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lampredotto"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Abomasum","ingredient/abomasum"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pasta Grannies","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pastagrannies.substack.com","note":"Vicky Bennison's long-running video series filming Italian grandmothers making regional pasta by hand.","url":"https://pastagrannies.substack.com/p/pasta-grannies-is-now-on-substack","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Recipes from Italy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"recipesfromitaly.com","note":"Traditional Italian recipes written by Italian home cooks, organized by region and course.","url":"https://recipesfromitaly.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Manu's Menu","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"manusmenu.com","note":"Italian-born home cook Manuela sharing classic family and regional Italian dishes.","url":"https://www.manusmenu.com/about/about-this-site","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Italian cuisine","links":[["Acquacotta","dish/acquacotta"],["Agliata","dish/agliata"],["Agnolotti","dish/agnolotti"],["Antipasto","dish/antipasto"],["Arancini","dish/arancini"],["Biscotti","dish/biscotti"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bombolone","dish/bombolone"],["Budino","dish/budino"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Cannelloni","dish/cannelloni"],["Capellini","dish/capellini"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Carpaccio","dish/carpaccio"],["Cassatella di sant'Agata","dish/cassatella-di-sant-agata"],["Cassoeula","dish/cassoeula"],["Ciabatta","dish/ciabatta"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Coda alla vaccinara","dish/coda-alla-vaccinara"],["Colomba pasquale","dish/colomba-pasquale"],["Conti roll","dish/conti-roll"],["Cotoletta alla bolognese","dish/cotoletta-alla-bolognese"],["Crostata","dish/crostata"],["Drago restaurants","dish/drago-restaurants"],["Focaccia pugliese","dish/focaccia-pugliese"],["Frittata","dish/frittata"],["Giardiniera","dish/giardiniera"],["Guanciale","dish/guanciale"],["Italian restaurant","dish/italian-restaurant"],["Italian wedding soup","dish/italian-wedding-soup"],["Lasagna","dish/lasagna"],["List of Italian restaurants","dish/list-of-italian-restaurants"],["Marinara sauce","dish/marinara-sauce"],["Mondeghili","dish/mondeghili"],["Paccheri","dish/paccheri"],["Panini (sandwich)","dish/panini-sandwich"],["Panna cotta","dish/panna-cotta"],["Passatelli","dish/passatelli"],["Pecorino","dish/pecorino"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/ca/Lampredotto_Sandwich.jpg/500px-Lampredotto_Sandwich.jpg","w":500,"h":397,"by":"William Held","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lampredotto_Sandwich.jpg"}},"ingredient/doubanjiang":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/doubanjiang","n":"Doubanjiang","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Doubanjiang, also known as douban, toban-djan, broad bean chili sauce, or fermented chili bean paste, is a hot and savory Chinese bean paste made from fermented broad beans (fava beans), chili peppers, soybeans, salt, and flour. Characteristically used in Sichuan cuisine, it has been called \"the soul of Sichuan cuisine.\" Sichuan dishes such as mapo tofu, huoguo (Sichuan hotpot), yuxiang flavoring, and shuizhu all use doubanjiang as a key ingredient. Other regions have their own versions: in Guangdong and Taiwan, for instance, Sichuan doubanjiang is called la-doubanjiang (Chinese: 辣豆瓣醬, \"la\" (辣) meaning \"hot\" or \"spicy\") to distinguish it from plainer  and milder versions."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-doubanjiang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doubanjiang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Mapo tofu","dish/mapo-tofu"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Douchi","ingredient/douchi"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e9/Doubanjiang.jpg/500px-Doubanjiang.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Badagnani","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doubanjiang.jpg"}},"ingredient/canning":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/canning","n":"Canning","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Canning is a method of food preservation in which food is processed and sealed in an airtight container (jars like Mason jars, and steel and tin cans). Canning provides a shelf life that typically ranges from one to five years, although under specific circumstances, it can be much longer. A freeze-dried canned product, such as canned dried lentils, could last as long as 30 years in an edible state."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-canning"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canning"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine",[["Mimosa salad","dish/mimosa-salad"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Mimosa salad","dish/mimosa-salad"]]],["Soviet cuisine","cuisine/soviet-cuisine",[["Mimosa salad","dish/mimosa-salad"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Mimosa salad","dish/mimosa-salad"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Canned fish","ingredient/canned-fish"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Grated cheese","ingredient/grated-cheese"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Thunnus","ingredient/thunnus"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d4/FMIB_34482_Making_Salmon_Cans.jpeg/500px-FMIB_34482_Making_Salmon_Cans.jpeg","w":500,"h":387,"by":"John Nathan Cobb","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FMIB_34482_Making_Salmon_Cans.jpeg"}},"dish/mujaddara":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mujaddara","n":"Mujaddara","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Cypriot cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Mujaddara is a dish consisting of cooked lentils together with groats, generally rice, and garnished with sautéed onions. It is especially popular in the Levant."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mujaddara"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mujaddara"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cypriot cuisine","cuisine/cypriot-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":8,"links":[["Bulgur","ingredient/bulgur"],["Carum","ingredient/carum"],["Lens","ingredient/lens"],["Lentil","ingredient/lentil"],["Lentils","ingredient/lentils"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Kopiaste","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kopiaste.org","note":"Long-running blog of Greek and Cypriot home recipes.","url":"https://www.kopiaste.org/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Cypriot cuisine","links":[["Fasolada","dish/fasolada"],["Stuffed squash","dish/stuffed-squash"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c9/Mujaddara.jpg/500px-Mujaddara.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Bazel","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mujaddara.jpg"}},"dish/nuoc-cham":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/nuoc-cham","n":"Nước chấm","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Vietnamese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Nước chấm, or more specifically nước mắm chấm for the fish-sauce version, is a Vietnamese dipping sauce often served as a condiment. It is a sweet, sour, salty, savoury sauce that often (but not always) contains bird's eye chili for spiciness."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-nuoc-cham"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Bc_ch%E1%BA%A5m"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Vietnamese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Viet World Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vietworldkitchen.com","note":"Andrea Nguyen's blog on Vietnamese cooking, ingredients and technique.","url":"https://vietworldkitchen.com/blog/category/recipes-vietnamese","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hungry Huy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hungryhuy.com","note":"Vietnamese-American Huy Vu's recipes for home-style Vietnamese dishes.","url":"https://www.hungryhuy.com/vietnamese/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Vietnamese cuisine","links":[["Bánh bao","dish/banh-bao"],["Bánh bột lọc","dish/banh-bot-loc"],["Bánh giầy","dish/banh-giay"],["Bánh tráng","dish/banh-trang"],["Chả rươi","dish/cha-ruoi"],["Chả trứng","dish/cha-trung"],["Chạo tôm","dish/chao-tom"],["Coconut candy","dish/coconut-candy"],["Cốm","dish/com"],["Khô cá lóc","dish/kho-ca-loc"],["Mắm nêm","dish/mam-nem"],["Ốc vú nàng","dish/oc-vu-nang"],["Shaking beef","dish/shaking-beef"],["Tương","dish/tuong"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/49/N%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Bc_m%E1%BA%AFm_%E1%BB%9Bt_20201127.jpg/500px-N%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Bc_m%E1%BA%AFm_%E1%BB%9Bt_20201127.jpg","w":500,"h":540,"by":"Phương Huy","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0